This finding reports user-controlled data reaching a server-side network request. A dynamic URL is not automatically SSRF; the final destination, normalization, redirects, DNS behavior, and credential forwarding must be proven.
1. Who controls the request URL, baseURL, path components, proxy settings, redirect targets, and DNS resolution?
2. Show the complete flow from the external source to the exact network-request operation.
3. Compute the final URL actually sent on the wire after base URL combination, URL parsing, encoding, proxy handling, and redirects.
4. Can an absolute URL override or bypass an intended base destination or allowlist?
5. Are loopback, private, link-local, cloud-metadata, file, alternate-scheme, and IPv6-transition destinations blocked after normalization and after each redirect?
6. Is validation applied to the resolved IP address, and is DNS rebinding considered?
7. Can credentials, cookies, authorization headers, proxy credentials, or other sensitive headers be forwarded to an attacker-selected host?
8. Is allowAbsoluteUrls false, or is the integrating application incorrectly relying on baseURL as a destination allowlist?
Use the tools to retrieve every caller, URL builder, redirect handler, proxy helper, configuration default, and module-level binding needed to prove the final destination and privileges.
Return a vulnerability only if an attacker can influence the destination and reach a security-sensitive network resource or exfiltrate sensitive data. Do not report expected HTTP-client behavior without a real trust-boundary violation.
